---
import { APP_STORE_IOS, APP_STORE_MAC } from "../lib/store";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<section class="bg-surface-50 px-6 py-24 md:py-32" id="apps">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-landing">
    <!-- Asymmetric header: headline left, statement right -->
    <div class="reveal grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-12 md:gap-16">
      <div class="md:col-span-7">
        <h2
          class="text-3xl font-bold leading-[1.15] tracking-tight text-ink md:text-[2.75rem]"
        >
          On your Mac. In your pocket.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="md:col-span-5">
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-light">
          The server, the web app, and the PWA are free and open source.
          <strong class="text-ink">The native Mac and iPhone apps are the
          one thing that costs money</strong> - buying them is what funds
          the rest.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Mac desktop app -->
    <article class="reveal reveal-delay-1 mt-16">
      <figure>
        <img
          src={`${base}screenshots/native-mac-imbox.png`}
          alt="The native Kurir Mac app showing the Imbox with personal mail in a sidebar layout"
          class="rounded-2xl border border-surface-200 shadow-2xl shadow-accent/5"
          width="2560"
          height="1676"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </figure>
      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-12 md:gap-16">
        <div class="md:col-span-5">
          <span
            class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-accent"
          >
            Paid &middot; on the Mac App Store
          </span>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-bold text-ink">The Mac app</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="md:col-span-7">
          <p class="leading-relaxed text-ink-light">
            A real Mac app. Same Swift as the iPhone, built for a keyboard
            and a wide window. Offline-first. Lists and threads read from a
            local store, and actions queue when you're offline.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-light">
            Search, shortcuts, a full native calendar, and the sidebar you
            already know. Paid, like the iPhone app, and what funds the rest
            of Kurir.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-5">
            <a
              href={APP_STORE_MAC}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-ink px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-medium text-white transition-colors hover:bg-accent"
            >
              Get the Mac app
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Two phone products, staggered -->
    <div class="mt-20 grid gap-16 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-12">
      <!-- Native iPhone app -->
      <div class="reveal reveal-delay-1">
        <figure class="mx-auto max-w-[300px]">
          <img
            src={`${base}screenshots/native-imbox.png`}
            alt="The native Kurir iPhone app showing the Imbox with personal mail"
            class="rounded-[2rem] border border-surface-200 shadow-2xl shadow-accent/10"
            width="780"
            height="1687"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </figure>
        <div class="mx-auto mt-10 max-w-md">
          <span
            class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-accent"
          >
            Paid &middot; on the App Store
          </span>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-bold text-ink">
            The native iPhone app
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-light">
            Real Swift, not a wrapped web page. Offline-first — every list
            and thread reads instantly from a local store, and actions queue
            up when you're off the grid. A native calendar with RSVP built
            in, and push notifications the moment mail lands in your Imbox.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-light">
            The native apps are the only part of Kurir you can pay for, and
            you never have to. Think of them as the project's tip jar -
            with a really nice app attached.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-5">
            <a
              href={APP_STORE_IOS}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-ink px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-medium text-white transition-colors hover:bg-accent"
            >
              Get the iPhone app
            </a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- PWA -->
      <div class="reveal reveal-delay-2 md:mt-20">
        <figure class="mx-auto max-w-[300px]">
          <img
            src={`${base}screenshots/pwa-feed.png`}
            alt="The Kurir PWA on a phone showing The Feed with newsletters"
            class="rounded-[2rem] border border-surface-200 shadow-2xl shadow-accent/10"
            width="780"
            height="1600"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </figure>
        <div class="mx-auto mt-10 max-w-md">
          <span
            class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-ink-muted"
          >
            Free &middot; iOS &amp; Android
          </span>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-2xl font-bold text-ink">
            The PWA
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-light">
            Every feature is here. Screener, Imbox, Feed, Paper Trail,
            search, compose - the same Kurir, installed from your own
            server. No App Store, no extra account. Full-screen, with
            push on iOS 16.4+ and Android.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-light">
            It's just not as smooth as the native iPhone app. The Swift
            client is offline-first and faster. The PWA is free, like
            everything else on your server.
            <a
              href="docs/mobile-app"
              class="font-medium text-accent underline decoration-accent/30 underline-offset-2 transition-colors hover:text-accent-dark hover:decoration-accent"
            >Install guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
